---
description: DocForge core architecture invariants (CDOM, boundaries, messaging)
alwaysApply: true
---

docforge-architecture:

- Read `docs/PROJECT_SPEC.md` and `docs/CURSOR_INSTRUCTIONS.md` before implementing major components.
- Do not change the architecture without explicit approval.
- CDOM is the only contract between providers and exporters. Never bypass CDOM.
- Go API must not import OCR frameworks or concrete provider implementations.
- Exporters must only consume CDOM; never OCR/layout provider types.
- Domain must not import infrastructure.
- Keep HTTP handlers thin; put use cases in application services.
- Use dependency injection and small interfaces.
- Prefer simple implementations over speculative abstractions.
- CPU-only; do not require NVIDIA GPU.
- Preserve ARM64 and x86_64 compatibility.
- Keep Python isolated to AI/OCR worker responsibilities unless a native Go path is deliberately chosen.
- Do not introduce Kubernetes, Kafka, or micro-frontends unless explicitly requested.
- RabbitMQ and Redis are approved for MVP:
  - RabbitMQ = job/stage work queue, retries, DLQ
  - Redis = progress cache / short-lived coordination / rate-limit helpers
  - PostgreSQL = durable source of truth for documents, jobs, artifacts
  - S3-compatible storage = PDFs, page images, CDOM JSON, export artifacts
- Do not store large PDF binaries in PostgreSQL.
- Providers must not update DB/job state directly or bypass the orchestrator.
